This example demonstrates three core components of a CUDA program:
• Host Code: Manages initialization, memory allocation, kernel launch, and post-processing. This is written in conventional C/C++ and runs on the CPU. • Memory Management: Because host and device use separate memory spaces, developers must explicitly allocate memory with cudaMalloc(), and transfer data between host and device using cudaMemcpy(). • Device (GPU) Kernel: Implements the parallel logic. The vectorAdd function uses built-in CUDA thread identifiers like threadIdx, blockIdx, and blockDim to distribute work across thousands of threads.
